    summary_en: "This article details the successful operation of the AMD openSIL and Coreboot firmware stack on modern server hardware. The affected product is the Gigabyte MZ33-AR1 motherboard, paired with the EPYC 9005 series processor. Through the efforts of 3mdeb, openSIL and Coreboot were successfully ported to the Gigabyte server motherboard supporting EPYC 9005, allowing users to directly flash Dasharo (a downstream version of Coreboot) from the BMC web interface. This significantly enhances server firmware transparency, allowing users to transition from proprietary BIOS to an open-source Coreboot+openSIL system. Although AMD officially anticipates openSIL's production readiness for Zen 6, this implementation demonstrates its feasibility on the existing EPYC 9005 platform. The article notes that this advancement means open-source firmware is no longer limited to older Opteron platforms, providing a more secure and transparent boot environment for modern servers, and laying the groundwork for future security and confidential computing requirements.",
