    summary_en: "This article deeply analyzes the status and development trends of the Google Pixel series in the current smartphone market. Pixel is viewed as the representative of Google's 'pure Android experience,' inheriting the tradition of the Nexus series and offering a user interface closest to native Android. Although Pixel has a low global market share, its 'native experience' positioning remains a core selling point. In terms of hardware, Pixel uses self-developed Tensor chips. Early versions (such as Tensor G1) faced issues with overheating, power consumption, and signal modules, and their performance still lags behind industry-leading chips (such as Snapdragon). However, with the Pixel 10 adopting Tensor G5, many major issues have been improved. The article points out that Pixel's success does not rely entirely on raw specifications but is built upon a 'unique experience' and 'brand positioning,' which allows Google to maintain market discussion. Consumers are advised to understand that Pixel's core value lies in its software ecosystem and system integration, rather than merely top-tier hardware specifications.",
